\begin{frontmatter}

\title{VMAF-Aware Runtime Shielding for Constrained\\ Deep Reinforcement Learning in Adaptive Video Streaming:\\ A Strictly Pareto-Improving Projection}

\author[uta]{Saeed Zarbi}
\author[uta]{Leili Farzinvash}
\author[uta]{Pedram Salehpour\corref{c1}}
\cortext[c1]{Corresponding author}
\ead[c1]{psalehpour@tabrizu.ac.ir}

\address[uta]{Department of Computer Engineering, Faculty of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Tabriz, Tabriz, Iran}

\begin{abstract}
Client-side Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) control must consistently balance perceptual video quality against the risk of playback stalls.
While purely reward-maximizing Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) can achieve strong average surrogate scores, it frequently learns aggressive bitrate policies that inflate tail stall risk under throughput volatility.
Conversely, conventional runtime safety projections (``shields'') layered on top of such policies often guarantee stability only at the \emph{unauditable} expense of visual quality, typically by forcing opaque uniform index decrements that are hard for operators to retune.

We formulate DASH/HLS bitrate selection as a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) that maximizes expected perceptual fidelity (VMAF in our harness) subject to empirical budgets on rebuffer accumulation and smoothed quality switching, instantiated with primal--dual Lagrangian relaxation and Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) together with manifest lookahead and buffer-stabilization cues.
On top of the learned policy we introduce a \emph{VMAF-aware runtime shield}: a deterministic action-projection layer that, whenever emergency step-down is required, navigates the bitrate ladder using \emph{non-linear, content-dependent} VMAF spacing (with an explicit VMAF-loss budget and a tunable catastrophic-ratio knob) rather than naive index-only decrements.

Extensive trace-driven replay on a standard FCC/Norway-style partitioned suite ($n{=}\VNepisodes$ paired episodes on identical policy and seeds) shows that the proposed architecture \emph{Pareto-improves} legacy index-decrement shielding in mean QoE, rebuffering, and VMAF, with a strictly significant triple at tighter VMAF-loss tolerance $\tau{=}0.8$ and a marginal VMAF $p$-value at the recommended $\tau{=}1.0$.
For example, at VMAF-loss tolerance $\tau{=}0.8$, the VMAF-aware shield suppresses mean session rebuffering from $\VRbOff{}\,\%$ without the shield to $\VRbVmAwareEpt{}\,\%$, compared with $\VRbLegacy{}\,\%$ for legacy projection, while simultaneously raising mean VMAF ($\VVmVmAwareEpt{}$ vs.\ $\VVmLegacy{}$) and mean session QoE ($\VQoVmAwareEpt{}$ vs.\ $\VQoLegacy{}$).
At the recommended $\tau{=}1.0$ operating point, paired Wilcoxon tests confirm large QoE and rebuffer gains versus legacy ($p{=}\VPairedPQoEvsLeg$, $p{=}\VPairedPRbvsLeg$) with a mean VMAF improvement whose two-sided $p$-value is marginal ($p{=}\VPairedPVMvsLeg$; $n{=}\VNepisodes$). Tightening to $\tau{=}0.8$ yields a strictly significant triple (\VPairedDQoEvsLegStr{} $\Delta$QoE, $\VPairedDRbvsLegStr{}\,\%$ rebuffer, $+\VPairedDVMvsLegStr$ VMAF; $p{=}\VPairedPQoEvsLegStr$, $p{=}\VPairedPRbvsLegStr$, $p{=}\VPairedPVMvsLegStr$).
Across $\VShieldVariants$ shield configurations the sweep empirically collapses to only $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes, isolating the VMAF-aware fallback as the dominant Pareto driver.

We further stress-test the same policy on an \emph{out-of-distribution} suite of synthetic \textbf{5G/mmWave-oriented stress traces} with intermittent outages: mean rebuffering grows to $\approx\VFiveGRbShieldOff\,\%$ without protection, while VMAF-aware projection preserves large paired QoE lifts and statistically significant rebuffer reductions relative to legacy projection (e.g.\ $\VFiveGPairedDQoEten$--$\VFiveGPairedDQoE$ $\Delta$QoE; $\VFiveGPairedDRbten\,\%$--$\VFiveGPairedDRb$ percentage-point $\Delta$rebuffer; $n{=}\VNepisodes$); paired mean VMAF is not cleanly resolved there, as expected when sessions become stall-saturated.

Removing the shield at inference from a shield-co-trained policy inflates rebuffering by $|{\VPairedDRbvsOff}|\,\%$ ($p{=}\VPairedPRbvsOff$), underscoring policy--shield co-design.
Relative to Pensieve and RobustMPC under the same harness, the recommended operating point reduces mean rebuffering by $\VRbReductionVsPen\,\%$ and $\VRbReductionVsMPC\,\%$, respectively.
Overall, the VMAF-aware projection is a quality-preserving, statistically grounded safety layer for constrained DRL ABR, validated on broadband partitions and corroborated under mobile-oriented outage stress.
\end{abstract}

