\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Deep client-side \emph{adaptive bitrate} (ABR) policies trained with reinforcement learning can track high average surrogate rewards, yet their \textbf{greedy tail behavior} is precisely what mobile deployments fear~\cite{mao2017neural,sutton2018reinforcement}.
Sub-6~GHz and especially \textbf{5G mmWave} access routinely inject millisecond-to-second capacity cliffs from blockage, LOS/NLOS transitions, scheduling, and mobility~\cite{meng2024mmwave,cisco2023}.
Under such volatility, unconstrained Deep RL (DRL) often remains \emph{bitrate-hungry} long enough to convert rare throughput dips into \textbf{long tail stalls} that dominate viewer Quality of Experience (QoE) even when mean logs look acceptable~\cite{wu2024qoe,yan2019learning}.
\textbf{The opening crisis is operational, not academic:} aggressive learned controllers can ``win'' average scores while silently concentrating probability mass on catastrophic rebuffer events~\cite{mao2017neural}.

Industry-grade stacks therefore bolt on \emph{runtime shields}---deterministic action projections executed immediately before each chunk download~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.
\textbf{The dominant legacy design is \textit{content blind}.}
When buffer-depletion risk triggers, conventional shields---including the index-decrement projection we inherit as a V12-era baseline---almost always apply \textbf{representation-index decrements}: step down one ladder rung, repeat until a crude viability test passes~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding,mao2017neural}.
Because perceptual fidelity is \textbf{not} affine in discrete bitrate indices, those decrements routinely \emph{over-pay} in VMAF for modest safety gains: adjacent ladder steps can differ by a few VMAF points or by ten, depending on content and encoding~\cite{li2016vmaf}.
\textbf{Legacy shielding is therefore a stopgap:} it enforces a crude safety--quality \emph{trade-off}---operators accept opaque visual damage to avoid stalls---even when a safer downgrade path with negligible perceptual loss exists on the same manifest~\cite{xiao2025qoe,tian2022towards}.

\textbf{We change the paradigm: the shield is itself an optimization layer, not merely a panic brake.}
Concretely, we introduce a \textbf{VMAF-aware runtime shield}: a deterministic projection rule that, whenever a catastrophic-download predicate fires, searches downward along the \emph{manifest-known} bitrate ladder under explicit buffer-margin viability constraints while maximizing retained VMAF subject to a perceptual-loss budget $\tau$ and a tunable catastrophic-ratio knob $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$ (Sec.~\ref{sec:method:shield}).
The logic is \textbf{analytically simple yet perceptual}: it is a transparent, auditable function of chunk sizes, conservative throughput surrogates, buffer seconds, and per-chunk VMAF tables---not another opaque neural head~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding,li2016vmaf}.
\textbf{Our method does not force operators to forfeit perceptual fidelity for safety by default;} it replaces blind index hopping with ladder-respecting downgrade choices that preserve as much VMAF as emergencies allow.

\textbf{Headline evidence: paired Pareto gains with rigorous statistics, not another hand-tuned compromise.}
Holding the trained policy, videos, and trace seeds fixed, we pair $n{=}\VNepisodes$ replay episodes and test legacy projection against VMAF-aware projection.
\textbf{On the standard FCC/Norway-style partitioned throughput suite} central to trace-driven ABR research, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests show \textbf{simultaneous mean improvements} in QoE, rebuffering, and VMAF versus legacy on paired episodes, with QoE and rebuffering highly significant at $\tau{=}1.0$ ($p{=}\VPairedPQoEvsLeg$, $p{=}\VPairedPRbvsLeg$) and VMAF marginal ($p{=}\VPairedPVMvsLeg$; Sec.~\ref{sec:eval}).
Tightening $\tau$ from $1.0$ to $0.8$ yields a \textbf{strict triple} significant at conventional $\alpha{=}0.05$ (\VPairedDQoEvsLegStr{} QoE, \(\VPairedDRbvsLegStr{}\,\%\) rebuffer, $+\VPairedDVMvsLegStr$ VMAF; $p{=}\VPairedPQoEvsLegStr$, $\VPairedPRbvsLegStr$, $\VPairedPVMvsLegStr$).
\textbf{We then stress the identical policy on synthetic 5G/mmWave-style outage traces} ($n{=}\VNepisodes$ paired replay) to mimic blockage-driven volatility~\cite{meng2024mmwave}: unshielded operation collapses into massive rebuffering, whereas VMAF-aware projection retains \textbf{large paired QoE gains and highly significant rebuffer reductions versus legacy} (Sec.~\ref{sec:eval:ood}).
There, session-mean VMAF tracks legacy within fractions of a point and two-sided Wilcoxon tests do not separate paired VMAF at conventional levels---an expected artifact when playback becomes stall-saturated rather than a contradiction of the primary strict-Pareto certificate~\cite{lakens2018equivalence}.
Finally, shield-design sweeps collapse $\VShieldVariants$ configurations into only $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes dominated by the VMAF-aware fallback, and removing the shield at inference from a shield-co-trained policy inflates rebuffering by $|{\VPairedDRbvsOff}|\,\%$ ($p{=}\VPairedPRbvsOff$), proving empirical \textbf{policy--shield co-design}~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.

This paper contributes the following:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{CMDP formulation and Lagrangian PPO training.} We cast ABR as a Constrained Markov Decision Process with primal--dual updates over stall and switching surrogates, Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), manifest lookahead, and buffer-regularization cues~\cite{altman1999constrained,paternain2019dual,schulman2017proximal,sodagar2011mpeg,neely2010stochastic}.

  \item \textbf{VMAF-aware runtime shielding.} We design a deterministic projection layer that optimizes emergency downgrade paths along nonlinear perceptual ladders instead of naive index decrements, with auditable predicates and operator-facing knobs $\tau$ and $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$ (Sec.~\ref{sec:method:shield}).

  \item \textbf{Paired statistical evidence.} Wilcoxon tests on $n{=}\VNepisodes$ episodes establish significant QoE and rebuffer gains vs.\ legacy at $\tau{=}1.0$, a strictly significant triple at $\tau{=}0.8$, and synthetic 5G/mmWave stress replay corroborates large QoE and stall reductions (Sec.~\ref{sec:eval}--\ref{sec:eval:ood}).

  \item \textbf{Inference co-design evidence.} We show empirically that constrained policies learn bitrate proposals whose safety is conditional on the deterministic shield at deployment time ($|\VPairedDRbvsOff|\,\%$ paired stall inflation when the shield is removed; $p{=}\VPairedPRbvsOff$)~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding,mao2017neural}.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Road map.}
Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related ABR and safe RL literature; Section~\ref{sec:system} formalizes the QoE surrogate, CMDP, and shield interfaces; Section~\ref{sec:method} details Lagrangian PPO and VMAF-aware projection; Section~\ref{sec:eval} presents paired statistics, tables, and figures (primary suite plus 5G/mmWave stress); Sections~\ref{sec:limits}--\ref{sec:conclusion} discuss limitations and conclusions.

