\textbf{Headline finding (RQ1).}
On the FCC/Norway-style primary suite (\(n{=}\VNepisodes\) paired episodes), toggling only the VMAF-aware fallback (fixed catastrophic-ratio knob) raises mean session QoE by \VPairedDQoEvsLeg, cuts mean rebuffering by $|\VPairedDRbvsLeg|\,\%$, and raises mean VMAF by \VPairedDVMvsLeg{} versus legacy projection on the same policy and seeds.
Paired Wilcoxon tests are highly significant for QoE and rebuffer ($p{=}\VPairedPQoEvsLeg$, $p{=}\VPairedPRbvsLeg$) at $\tau{=}1.0$, while VMAF is marginal ($p{=}\VPairedPVMvsLeg$).
At $\tau{=}0.8$, the same comparison yields \VPairedDQoEvsLegStr\, $\Delta$QoE, $\VPairedDRbvsLegStr\,\%$ rebuffering, and $+\VPairedDVMvsLegStr$ VMAF with all three $p$-values below $0.05$---a \emph{strict} triple in the usual sense.
To our knowledge, no prior ABR shield documents this paired mean triple together with strictly significant nonparametric tests at comparable harness fidelity.

\textbf{(OOD stress traces.)}
Section~\ref{sec:eval:ood} repeats the replay under synthetic 5G/mmWave outages; QoE gains and stall reductions versus the legacy shield remain paired-significant (e.g., \(\tau{=}1.0\): \(\VFiveGPairedDQoEten\) $\Delta$QoE with \(p{=}\VFiveGPairedPQoEten\); \(\VFiveGPairedDRbten\,\%\) points $\Delta$rebuffer with \(p{=}\VFiveGPairedPRbten\); Table~\ref{tab:wilcoxon:5g:stress}). Mean VMAF is not reliably separated (\(p=\VFiveGPairedPVmten\) at \(\tau{=}1.0\)), which we interpret as limited statistical power once average session VMAF collapses toward a stall-dominated plateau rather than contradictory evidence relative to Table~\ref{tab:pareto:family}.

Table~\ref{tab:pareto:family} reports the full sweep. The VMAF-aware family (rows 2--5) Pareto-dominates the legacy anchor on QoE, rebuffering, \emph{and} VMAF concurrently. By contrast, threshold-only sweeps that vary $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$ \emph{without} the VMAF-aware fallback (the third block of the table) move QoE up by smaller, statistically non-significant margins while \emph{worsening} rebuffering by $2$--$5$ percentage points (Section~\ref{sec:eval:shields} details). This isolates the VMAF-aware fallback as the active Pareto driver: it is the \emph{shape} of the projection rule, not the threshold itself, that bends the front.

