\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\label{sec:limits}
\textbf{Scope of the claim.}
The contribution of this paper is a \emph{deployable, auditable QoE-vs-stall trade space}: runtime shields parametrized by a single catastrophic-ratio knob, with a per-video VMAF-aware fallback, that operators can tune post-deployment without retraining. We do not prove formal worst-case safety for arbitrary future networks, and we do not claim scalar-QoE dominance over every ABR baseline at every operating point. We claim, and statistically verify, that (i)~the family Pareto-improves over a standard projection shield at matched rebuffer budgets, (ii)~it dominates RobustMPC in mean rebuffering across all sweep points while remaining within paired distance of Pensieve at recommended settings, and (iii)~the design space collapses to a low-dimensional family controlled chiefly by the catastrophic ratio.

\textbf{Threats to validity.}
First, the evaluation is simulation-based and may not capture live cellular/mmWave dynamics, TCP/QUIC stack effects, CDN cache behavior, or player implementation details~\cite{meng2024mmwave}.
Second, the test set contains a finite collection of videos and network traces, so larger-scale trace diversity and stratified reporting would be needed before deployment claims~\cite{kvalbein2014}.
Third, QoE is measured with a VMAF-based surrogate and VMAF-difference smoothness penalty; subjective user studies could rank stall, fidelity, and switching discomfort differently~\cite{wu2024qoe}.
Fourth, the structural finding that the shield-design space collapses into $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes is conditioned on \emph{this} policy, ladder, and trace mix; alternative ladders (e.g., HDR with widely non-uniform VMAF gaps) could expand the discriminating power of the VMAF-aware fallback and warrant re-characterization.
Fifth, the policy/shield co-design dependence we surface (Sec.~\ref{sec:eval:codesign}) means that the recommended operating points are achievable only when the shipping client carries the shield; an operator who removes the shield silently is no longer at any of the points reported in Table~\ref{tab:pareto:family}.
Finally, Genie is an oracle reference with perfect future trace knowledge, so its gap should be read as diagnostic context rather than a practically attainable target.
Sixth, the synthetic 5G/mmWave outage suite isolates throughput shift but lacks stack realism; VMAF moreover ceases to be a sharp paired discriminator versus the legacy shield when sessions become overwhelmingly stall-limited, reinforcing that surrogate QoE and stall seconds remain the statistically reliable headline metrics there unless paired \(n\) is increased substantially or equivalence/non-inferiority tests are layered in~\cite{lakens2018equivalence}.

\textbf{Mitigation roadmap.}
Future work should evaluate the same Pareto-tunable shield family on live or packet-level emulation, add broader mobile/fixed-broadband trace families, report per-trace tail metrics in addition to means, and calibrate the catastrophic-ratio knob using deployment telemetry (e.g., per-CDN, per-region, per-time-of-day). Risk-sensitive objectives such as CVaR~\cite{rockafellar2000} and uncertainty-aware throughput prediction are natural extensions but should complement, not replace, explicit guard auditing. A particularly attractive direction is \emph{adaptive} catastrophic-ratio scheduling: rather than a single fixed knob, an outer controller could move along the Pareto front in response to observed live QoE budgets.

% =========================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
We repositioned adaptive streaming as \textbf{Lagrangian CMDP surrogate training} complemented by a \textbf{VMAF-aware Pareto-tunable runtime shield}: a deterministic action projection that, when the catastrophic-download test fires, selects the highest-VMAF safe rung subject to a small VMAF-loss budget rather than uniformly decrementing the bitrate index.
Under paired trace-driven evaluation on the primary FCC/Norway-style suite (Sec.~\ref{sec:eval}), the proposed shield is the first ABR shield to our knowledge that achieves a \emph{strict empirical three-way Pareto improvement} over the standard index-decrement projection on the same trained policy and identical trace seeds: paired QoE rises by \VPairedDQoEvsLeg{} ($p{=}\VPairedPQoEvsLeg$), paired rebuffering falls by $|\VPairedDRbvsLeg|\,\%$ ($p{=}\VPairedPRbvsLeg$), and paired VMAF rises by \VPairedDVMvsLeg{} ($p{=}\VPairedPVMvsLeg$, marginal at $\alpha{=}0.05$ for $\tau{=}1.0$). A tighter VMAF-loss budget yields a strictly significant triple at $\alpha{=}0.05$: $+\VPairedDQoEvsLegStr$ paired QoE, $\VPairedDRbvsLegStr\,\%$ rebuffering, and $+\VPairedDVMvsLegStr$ VMAF ($p{=}\VPairedPVMvsLegStr$). Supplementary synthetic 5G/mmWave stress traces (Sec.~\ref{sec:eval:ood}) preserve significant paired QoE and rebuffer shifts versus legacy while paired VMAF is not cleanly resolved; we interpret this bundle as corroborative tail-risk stress testing rather than a second venue for VMAF-hypothesis refinement.
A controlled sweep over $\VShieldVariants$ shield variants collapses into only $\VShieldBehaviors$ behavioral classes, identifying the VMAF-aware fallback (the projection \emph{shape}) rather than the catastrophic-ratio threshold (the projection \emph{level}) as the dominant Pareto driver.
We further document a strong policy/shield co-design dependence: removing the shield at inference inflates rebuffering by $|\VPairedDRbvsOff|\,\%$ ($p{=}\VPairedPRbvsOff$), arguing that constrained policies and their shields are operationally a single shippable unit.
The contribution is therefore a deployable, auditable, and \emph{empirically Pareto-improving} runtime shield (strict nonparametric triple at \(\tau{=}0.8\); recommended \(\tau{=}1.0\) retains strong QoE/rebuffer gains) with two interpretable deployment knobs (VMAF-loss tolerance and catastrophic ratio)---rather than yet another single-point ABR controller.
Future work targets differentiable risk metrics such as CVaR~\cite{rockafellar2000}, online prediction uncertainty, adaptive scheduling of the VMAF-loss tolerance from live telemetry, and live/packet-level validation.

